; =====================================================================
; cde-control-operator.cddl
;
; VERBATIM.  This is not ours.  The single rule between the markers below
; is reproduced byte-for-byte, including the document's three-space page
; margin, from:
;
;   draft-ietf-cbor-cde-13, "CBOR Common Deterministic Encoding (CDE)",
;   C. Bormann, Universitaet Bremen TZI, 14 October 2025,
;   Intended status: Best Current Practice, Updates: 8949 (if approved),
;   <https://www.ietf.org/archive/id/draft-ietf-cbor-cde-13.txt>
;
;   Section 4, "CDDL support".  Cached source line 915
;   (.cache/draft-ietf-cbor-cde.txt, sha256
;   bc554e3d2d37182f5f37c88aac2e61298089438068ccbc7c674d0940e675605c).
;
; THIS ONE LINE IS THE WHOLE OF THE DOCUMENT'S CDDL.  draft-ietf-cbor-cde-13
; defines no CDDL grammar for CDE itself -- and says why: Section 4 opens
; "CDDL defines the structure of CBOR data items at the data model level
; [...] It does not specify encoding", and Section 3.1.2 adds that basing
; CDE on the generic data model "means that there is no effect on the
; Concise Data Definition Language (CDDL) [RFC8610], except where the data
; description is documenting specific encoding decisions for byte strings
; that carry embedded CBOR (see Section 4)".  That exception is exactly the
; rule below.  Appendix A.1 states the same thing from the other side:
; "Encoding constraints (unconstrained well-formed, preferred-
; serialization, definite-length-only, cde) are orthogonal to data-model-
; level data definitions as provided by [RFC8610]."
;
; Context, quoted from Section 4 (cached source lines 906-908 and 913-914):
;   "The control operators .cde and .cdeseq are exactly like .cbor and
;    .cborseq except that they also require the encoded data item(s) to be
;    encoded according to CDE."
;   "For example, a byte string of embedded CBOR that is to be encoded
;    according to CDE can be formalized as:"
;
; `leaf` is the document's own illustration of `.cde`.  `.cdeseq` is named
; in the prose and registered in Table 2 (Section 6) but is never exercised
; in any rule anywhere in the document.  Section 4 also records, in a
; to-be-removed editorial note at lines 910-911, that "there is no .dlo or
; .dloseq for definite-length-only, as, so far, a requirement for these
; hasn't been detected" -- do not assume such an operator exists.
;
; The machine-checkable renderings written for this record carry
; `.derived.` in their names.  See README.md.
;
; -- verbatim extract begins (cached source line 915) --
   leaf = #6.24(bytes .cde any)
; -- verbatim extract ends (cached source line 915) --
